package Vixen.Shaders.Water

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// Which screen tiles have any water in them — docs/plan/35 § D8's tile classification, and the last
// thing on doc 35's list.
//
// **§ D8 keeps this "for its actual reason: the water pass is expensive per pixel and covers a small
// fraction of most frames".** It is an optimisation over a pass that is already correct, which is why
// it was built last rather than first: a classification written before the look was proven would have
// been a second place for a wrong picture to come from.
//
// ⚠ **§ D8 says to classify from "the GBuffer's shading model id, which already exists" — it does
// not.** The deferred path is ⬜ and this engine is Forward+, so `WaterSurfacePass` writes a
// one-channel coverage mask instead, which is exactly what an id comparison would have produced. When
// the deferred path lands, `waterSurface` becomes that comparison and nothing else here changes.
//
// ⚠ **It reads the surface exactly the way the water pass's own fragment stage reads it** — point
// sampled, at the centre of a *target* pixel — rather than fetching the surface's texels. That is what
// makes the classification conservative by construction instead of by argument: a tile is claimed if
// and only if some pixel of it would have found coverage, whatever resolution the surface plane
// happens to be at. A classifier that walked the surface's own texels would agree only while the two
// planes were the same size, and would silently drop water the day one of them was not.
//
// **One workgroup per tile, one lane per pixel, and one bit of answer.** `VisibilityTiles.rvn` bins by
// material and needs a shared word per material; this needs a shared bit, so the whole reduction is one
// `atomicOr` and one write.

/// The tiling's shape, and the arithmetic the classifier and the water pass address tiles with.
///
/// Constants rather than permutations because the host, this shader and `Water.rvn`'s vertex stage all
/// have to agree about which tile an instance is — the same argument `VisibilityTile` makes.
struct WaterTile {
    /// How many pixels a tile is on a side.
    ///
    /// Eight, so a tile is sixty-four pixels and one workgroup classifies it with one lane each — the
    /// same size `VisibilityTile` uses, and for the same reason. Larger tiles claim more dry pixels
    /// around a shoreline; smaller ones cost more instances for the same water.
    const val Size = 8

    /// How many tiles cover a target of this many pixels.
    static func CountFor(size: int2): int2 => int2((size.x + Size - 1) / Size, (size.y + Size - 1) / Size)

    /// One tile's index in a row-major grid of them.
    static func Index(tile: int2, count: int2): int => tile.y * count.x + tile.x

    /// The tile a row-major index names — what an instance id means.
    static func Unindex(index: int, count: int2): int2 => int2(index % count.x, index / count.x)

    /// Which corner of a tile's two triangles a vertex of `Draw(6, tiles)` is, in 0..1.
    ///
    /// **Six vertices and not three, and the difference is the whole saving.** A screen-covering
    /// triangle is twice the viewport so that its visible quarter needs no clipping; scaled down to one
    /// tile the same triangle covers *four* tiles, and shading a water tile up to four times costs more
    /// than the dry tiles it skipped. Two triangles cover a tile exactly, and a rectangle is what a tile
    /// is.
    static func Corner(vertexIndex: int): float2 {
        // 0,1,2 and then 1,2,3 — the second triangle shares the first's diagonal, so the four corners
        // are (0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1) in that order and no pixel is covered twice.
        val corner = vertexIndex < 3 ? vertexIndex : vertexIndex - 2

        return float2(float(corner % 2), float(corner / 2))
    }
}

shader WaterTiles {
    /// The surface plane: device depth in `r`, and the coverage the surface pass wrote in `g`.
    var waterSurface: Texture2D

    /// Point, and it has to be — see this file's remarks. A filtered tap would find coverage half a
    /// texel outside the water and claim tiles the pass then shades for nothing.
    var pointSampler: Sampler

    /// One word per tile: non-zero where the water pass has something to do.
    ///
    /// **A flag per tile rather than a compacted list, and that is a decision rather than a shortcut.**
    /// A compacted list needs the count on the device to draw from, and `ICommandList` has
    /// `DrawIndexedIndirect` and no non-indexed `DrawIndirect` — so an indirect draw here means either a
    /// three-entry index buffer for a triangle that has no vertex buffer, or reading the count back to
    /// the host, which is a stall a frame long every frame to avoid a pass over tiles that are mostly
    /// empty. That is the cost this feature exists to remove, paid the other way round.
    ///
    /// What a flag costs instead is one *collapsed* triangle pair per dry tile — a vertex stage that
    /// reads one word and emits a degenerate rectangle. At 1080p that is thirty-two thousand instances
    /// the setup engine discards and no pixel work at all, against a full-screen pass of the most
    /// expensive fragment shader in the frame.
    var tiles: RWBuffer<uint>

    /// How many tiles cover the target, in x and y.
    var tileCount: int2

    /// And how many pixels the target is, which is what bounds the lanes at the right and bottom edges.
    var targetSize: int2

    /// Whether any pixel of this tile is water. One bit, shared by the workgroup.
    groupshared var covered: uint

    /// One workgroup per tile, one invocation per pixel of it.
    ///
    /// The dispatch is `(tileCount.x, tileCount.y, 1)` with a workgroup of `Size * Size`, so a tile is a
    /// workgroup exactly and a pixel is a lane. Lanes past the target's right or bottom edge — where the
    /// last tile of a row hangs over — read nothing and set no bit.
    [ComputeShader(64)]
    func Main([Semantic("SV_GroupID")] group: uint3, [Semantic("SV_GroupIndex")] lane: uint) {
        if (lane == 0u) {
            covered = 0u
        }

        barrier()

        val tile = int2(int(group.x), int(group.y))
        val pixel = tile * WaterTile.Size + int2(int(lane) % WaterTile.Size, int(lane) / WaterTile.Size)

        if (pixel.x < targetSize.x && pixel.y < targetSize.y) {
            // The centre of the pixel, which is where the interpolator puts the water pass's own `uv` —
            // so this tap and that one are the same fetch of the same texel.
            val centre = float2(float(pixel.x), float(pixel.y)) + float2(0.5f)
            val uv = centre / float2(float(targetSize.x), float(targetSize.y))

            if (waterSurface.SampleLevel(pointSampler, uv, 0f).g > 0f) {
                atomicOr(covered, 1u)
            }
        }

        barrier()

        // One lane writes, because a tile is claimed once however many of its pixels claimed it.
        if (lane == 0u) {
            tiles[WaterTile.Index(tile, tileCount)] = covered
        }
    }
}
